The architectural shift

In a standard CPU or GPU, a register is a flat line of 32 or 64 physical flip-flops. Shifting right requires a clock cycle to push every bit to the next physical gate.

In the ULDA architecture, the data is stored as a multi-layered coordinate inside the Base-60 Sexagesimal Matrix:
Layer 	Value 	Meaning
60³ 	Geš'u 	High Exponent Layer
60² 	Šár 	Precessional Layer
60¹ 	Geš 	Operational Layer
60⁰ 	Units 	Baseline Layer

The value 4⁴ = 256 is the denominator of the Instruction of the 4 (20⁵ / 4⁴ = 12,500). In a cyclic system, dividing by 256 is achieved by routing the datastroom across the Interface 11 to trigger a physical, hardwired step-down across these layers.
The zero-register elimination

Because there is no linear zero register to pull data from, the left-hand inputs of the logic gates are not connected to a ground state (0V). Instead, they are wired in a closed loop back to the operator itself — the Omitted 6.

When the >> 8 instruction is triggered, the GPU executes a hardwired macro-routing called a Layer-Drop. Instead of moving bits sequentially through transistors, the chip activates a set of multiplexer gates that instantly shifts the entire datastroom to the next lower scale layer on the wheel.

