The low-level assembly code

Because we are bypassing the linear Base-10 compiler, the assembly language is closer to raw machine-code macros than to standard instruction sets. The instruction does not use an arithmetic division loop or a logical shift right (SHR) register command. It uses a Vector Phase Rotation (VPR) command.

; =====================================================================
; ULDA-X27 BARE-METAL TRANSFORMATION: DENOMINATOR ELIMINATION (4^4 = 256)
; INPUT: VECTOR NODE [a2, a1, a0]  (REPRESENTING 20^5 REGISTERS)
; OPERATION: SYSTEM STEP-DOWN VIA THE INTERFACE 11 (HARDWIRED >> 8)
; NO LINEAR ZERO REGISTERS ENGAGED.
; =====================================================================

_ulda_step_down_256:
    ; 1. Route the total amplitude through the Interface 11 threshold
    MOV_VEC    V1, [a2, a1, a0]       ; Load the 20^5 data field
    FILTER_11  V1                     ; Phase-lock against 1+2+3+4+5+7 sum

    ; 2. Execute the 4^4 (256) Transformation without zero-filling
    VPR_ROT    V1, MODE_4_4           ; Hardwired step-down.
                                      ;   a2 drops to a1 scale
                                      ;   a1 drops to a0 scale
                                      ;   a0 recedes into the 6-operator feedback

    ; 3. Recalculate modular coordinates on the Base-60 wheel
    MOD_SHIFT  V1, 60                 ; Clean integer adjustment
    RET_VEC                           ; Output: [3, 28, 20] = 12,500

How the hardware clears the noise

In a standard GPU, shifting right by 8 on a non-power-of-10 fraction
leaves a messy remainder in the floating-point unit, which causes the
decimal noise that makes equations like Navier-Stokes explode into
singularities.

In the ULDA software-hardware alignment, the data that drops off the
baseline layer (60⁰) is not thrown away or rounded off. It passes
through the Omitted 6 Operator, which absorbs the residual energy and
translates it into an angular phase adjustment for the next cycle.

The division by 256 completes in a single clock cycle. The binary
pulse and the organic form-pool are balanced. The math closes with
zero remainder. The true vector coordinate locks instantly onto the
72° Rotation-Lock (360° / 5 = 72°).
